module Darklang.Cli.Docs.HttpClient

let content(): String =
  """# HTTP Client

## Requests

  Stdlib.HttpClient.get(url, [ ("Accept", "application/json") ])
  Stdlib.HttpClient.post(url, [ Stdlib.HttpClient.ContentType.json ], body)
  Stdlib.HttpClient.request("PATCH", url, headers, body)   # any method; body is Bytes
  Stdlib.HttpClient.stream("GET", url, headers, Stdlib.Bytes.empty)

Each answers `Result<Response<'body>, RequestError>`:

  type Stdlib.HttpClient.Response<'body> =
    { statusCode: Int64, headers: List<String * String>, body: 'body }

`body` is Bytes from `request`, `get` and the rest, and a lazy `Stream<UInt8>`
from `stream`. Header values come back as the server sent them, and a header
sent twice (`Set-Cookie`) is two pairs. Look one up whatever its case:

  Stdlib.HttpClient.Response.header(r, "ETag")       # Some("\"AbC\"")
  Stdlib.HttpClient.Response.headers(r, "Set-Cookie") # every value
  Stdlib.HttpClient.Response.text(r)                  # body as UTF-8, or Decode

A GET or HEAD with no body is a read: it runs while your program goes on, and
waits only where you look at the response, so `List.map(urls, get)` has them
all in flight at once. Every other request runs in program order.

## Middleware

A request is a record, and sending is a function:

  type Request = { method, url, headers, body, timeoutMs: Option<Int64> }
  type Send<'body> = Request -> Result<Response<'body>, RequestError>
  type Middleware<'body> = Send<'body> -> Send<'body>

`get` and friends send through `Stdlib.HttpClient.defaultMiddleware()`: trim header
names, refuse an empty one, and lowercase the response's names. Build your own
client from a list, first listed outermost:

  let send =
    Stdlib.HttpClient.client(
      Stdlib.List.flatten(
        [ Stdlib.HttpClient.defaultMiddleware()
          [ Stdlib.HttpClient.Middleware.baseUrl("https://api.example.com/v1/")
            Stdlib.HttpClient.Middleware.bearer("https://api.example.com", token)
            Stdlib.HttpClient.Middleware.timeout(5000)
            Stdlib.HttpClient.Middleware.followRedirects(5)
            Stdlib.HttpClient.Middleware.retry(3, 200) ] ]))

  send(Stdlib.HttpClient.Request { method = "GET", url = "users", headers = [],
    body = Stdlib.Bytes.empty, timeoutMs = Stdlib.Option.Option.None })

`client([])` is the raw builtin, `Stdlib.HttpClient.sendRaw`, which reshapes
nothing; `streamClient` is the same for streams.

  Middleware.onRequest(f)        rewrite every request
  Middleware.onResponse(f)       rewrite every response, without waiting for it
  Middleware.header(n, v)        add a header unless the request has one
  Middleware.timeout(ms)         the host gives up after ms: `Timeout`
  Middleware.baseUrl(base)       resolve relative urls against base
  Middleware.bearer(origin, t)   Authorization: Bearer, to that origin only
  Middleware.followRedirects(n)  301/302/303/307/308, then `TooManyRedirects`
  Middleware.retry(n, ms)        idempotent methods only, backing off from ms

A middleware is any function from a `Send` to a `Send`, so your own is a few
lines, used as `logged()` in the list:

  let logged(): Stdlib.HttpClient.Middleware<Bytes> =
    fun next ->
      fun req ->
        let r = next(req)
        Stdlib.printLine(req.method + " " + req.url)
        r

That one waits for the response where it is sent, and so do `followRedirects`
and `retry`: each has to look at the response to decide. A GET through them is
no longer in flight while your program goes on. `onResponse` does not wait: its
function runs when the response arrives (`Stdlib.whenReady`), which is why it
may only compute, and why the default stack keeps a GET concurrent.

## What middleware cannot do

Your grants, the network guard, and the refusal of CR, LF or NUL in a header
are the host's, below every middleware. A redirect or a retry is a fresh send,
checked again for wherever it goes. Traces record each send, so every hop and
every attempt shows in `traces show`.

## Errors

  BadUrl(...)  Timeout  BadHeader(...)  NetworkError  TlsError(msg)  BadMethod
  TooManyRedirects  Decode(msg)

`Stdlib.HttpClient.toString(e)` says one in words.
"""
